<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The Samsung ice maker frosts over — and a forced defrost resets it.</h2>

    <p><strong>If your Samsung French-door refrigerator stopped making ice, the ice maker has most likely frosted and iced over inside its compartment. It's a widely documented pattern: warm, humid air reaches the ice room, condenses on the sensors and mold, and freezes into a block that jams the harvest. The immediate reset is a full forced defrost — melt all the ice, then restart the ice maker and force a test cycle. If it re-freezes within a week or two, the underlying cause (a worn ice-room seal, the ice-room fan, or a sensor) still needs fixing. Error codes 39E/39C and 26E point straight at the ice maker. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>This is the most common Samsung ice maker call we get, and the frustrating part for owners is that a defrost fixes it for a couple of weeks and then it's back. That's the signature of a root cause that a defrost alone doesn't reach — which is exactly what a proper diagnosis sorts out.</p>

    <p>That said, not every Samsung refrigerator not making ice is a frost problem. If the ice room is dry and clear but there's still no ice, work the water path the way you would on any fridge — a clogged water filter (replace it every six months), a closed supply valve or kinked line, a frozen fill tube, or a failed water inlet valve. Our all-brand <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> guide covers that water-path checklist; the frost pattern below is what makes Samsung different.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 THE PATTERN + CLASS ACTION -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · The documented pattern — and the lawsuit facts</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Widespread, well-documented, and not your fault.</h2>

    <p>The frosting problem on Samsung French-door ice makers is well enough known that it became the subject of litigation. Here are the facts, stated accurately: a case called <em>Bianchi v. Samsung</em> was filed in federal court in New Jersey, alleging that certain Samsung ice makers were defective and froze over.</p>

    <p><strong>What it did not do is produce a nationwide settlement.</strong> The named claims were dismissed in late 2023 and the matter was resolved on an individual basis — so there is no class-wide payout and no claim form to file. Anyone telling you to "file for the Samsung ice maker settlement" is mistaken; there isn't one. What the case does establish is that the frosting pattern is real and widespread, not something you caused by how you use your refrigerator. For a working fridge, the practical route is a repair of your specific unit — not a legal claim.</p>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Why it frosts over</h3>
      <p>Warm, humid room air reaches the sealed ice-room compartment — usually past a duct seal or flap that no longer closes tightly — and condenses on the cold sensors and ice mold. Over days it builds into frost, then ice, until the ice maker can't rotate or sense correctly and stops harvesting. Southern California's humidity, especially near the coast, feeds the condensation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CODES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · What the codes mean</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung ice-maker error codes, in plain terms.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>39E / 39C — ice maker not completing its cycle</h3>
      <p>The control board sees the ice maker fail to finish a harvest. Usually a frozen or jammed ice maker. A forced defrost is the first thing to try.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>26E — ice maker function error</h3>
      <p>The ice maker isn't making ice. Check that it's switched on, the freezer is cold enough, and the compartment isn't frozen over before it's called a part failure.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>40E / 40C — ice-room fan</h3>
      <p>The fan that circulates cold air through the ice room has failed or is obstructed. A tech job.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>14E, 8E — ice-maker sensors · 33E — ice-pipe heater</h3>
      <p>Sensor or heater faults in the ice-making system. These point to a specific failed part rather than a defrost issue, and are tech-level replacements.</p>
    </div>

    <p>These meanings are documented on Samsung's own support pages. For the complete Samsung refrigerator code list — including non-fault states like <strong>OF OF</strong> (Cooling Off / demo mode) that owners often mistake for a breakdown — see our <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/samsung-error-codes/">Samsung refrigerator error codes</a> guide.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely do</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Force a defrost, reset, and watch it.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Do a full forced defrost.</strong> Empty the ice bin, turn the ice maker off, and either unplug the fridge or leave the ice compartment open for several hours to a day until all frost and ice have melted. Keep a towel down for runoff.</li>
      <li><strong>Use the model's forced-defrost mode if it has one.</strong> Many Samsung models enter a forced-defrost mode with a specific button combination — check your model's manual for the exact sequence, as it varies.</li>
      <li><strong>Restart and force a harvest.</strong> Turn the ice maker back on, confirm it's enabled in the settings, and press the Test button on the ice-maker unit to run a fresh cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the basics.</strong> Verify the freezer is cold enough to make ice, the water supply valve is open, and the water filter isn't overdue.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch for two weeks.</strong> If it stays clear, the defrost was enough. If it frosts over again, the seal, fan, or sensor is the real cause — that's the tech handoff.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> defrosting, resetting, and checking supply and temperature are all safe homeowner steps. Do not disassemble the ice-room ducting, replace the fan or sensors, or open the sealed refrigeration system yourself. Recurring frost means a component fix, which is our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + INTERNAL LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Frost back in two weeks means a real fix is needed.</h2>

    <p>When a forced defrost only buys a couple of weeks, our techs address what let the frost in: a worn ice-room duct seal or flap, a failed ice-room fan, or a sensor reading wrong. On models where the ice-maker assembly itself is the weak point, we replace the assembly with the correct part for your model. We verify the ice-room seal and airflow first, so you're not paying for a new fan when a $-few seal was letting humid air pour in.</p>

    <p>More Samsung help: the <a href="/brands/samsung/">Samsung appliance repair</a> pillar, <a href="/brands/samsung-refrigerator-repair/">Samsung refrigerator repair</a>, and <a href="/brands/samsung-ice-maker-repair/">Samsung ice maker repair</a>. If your ice maker isn't Samsung or isn't frosting, start with <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/not-making-ice/">ice maker not making ice</a> or the <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/">ice maker repair</a> hub. Built-in fridge ice makers are covered under <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/ice-maker-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/irvine/ice-maker-repair/">Irvine</a>, and <a href="/thousand-oaks/ice-maker-repair/">Thousand Oaks</a> in most cases. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
